## 19. RISK ASSESSMENT

### 19.1 HRMS Migration Risk Matrix

| Migration | Data Loss | Feature Break | User Impact | Overall |
|-----------|----------|--------------|-------------|---------|
| H1-H2 (departments, designations) | 🟢 None | 🟢 None | 🟢 None | 🟢 Low |
| H3-H4 (profiles restructure) | 🟡 Medium | 🔴 High | 🟡 Medium | 🔴 High |
| H8-H9 (leave system) | 🟡 Medium | 🟡 Medium | 🟡 Medium | 🟡 Medium |
| H10-H11 (attendance) | 🟡 Medium | 🟡 Medium | 🟡 Medium | 🟡 Medium |
| H13-H14 (payroll) | 🟡 Medium | 🟢 Low | 🟢 Low | 🟡 Medium |
| H16-H17 (performance) | 🔴 High | 🟡 Medium | 🟡 Medium | 🔴 High |
| H21 (drop deprecated columns) | 🔴 High | 🔴 High | 🟡 Medium | 🔴 Critical |

### 19.2 Critical Mitigations

| Risk | Mitigation |
|------|-----------|
| Department name variations (Engg vs Engineering) | Pre-audit DISTINCT values; present cleanup list to HR for approval |
| `team_lead_id` → `reporting_manager_id` rename breaks queries | Deploy column rename + code update in same transaction |
| Performance history JSONB → table migration | Parse each entry; validate date/score integrity; keep JSONB as backup for 90 days |
| Dropping `profiles.payroll_ctc` breaks Payroll.tsx | Payroll.tsx must be refactored to read `salary_details` BEFORE column drop |
| Employee sees broken dashboard after migration | Feature flags: dual-read from old + new tables during transition |

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This HRMS execution plan introduces **17 new tables** and restructures the `profiles` table that is referenced by **25+ frontend files**. The profiles restructure (H3-H4, H21) is the highest-risk migration and must be coordinated with the frontend refactor. Deploy the ATS migrations (Phase 6) first, then HRMS migrations, to avoid conflicting schema changes.

> [!CAUTION]
> **Migration H21 (drop deprecated columns) is irreversible.** It must be the LAST migration executed, after ALL frontend files have been updated to use the new table structure. Full `pg_dump` backup required before execution. 90-day rollback window with backup retention.
